"Encodes directed quantities through nodes with link widths proportional to flow magnitude.\n\nUse when: A conserved or explicitly balanced quantity moves among sources, stages, and destinations. The main question concerns dominant contributions and losses.\n\nAvoid when: Links lack quantitative weights or flow balance cannot be defined.\n\nVisual grammar: Node position identifies stage or role rather than a measured coordinate. Link thickness encodes flow; node extent encodes a declared incoming or outgoing total.\n\nBuild: Define node identities, weighted links, units, and stage order. Reconcile inflows, outflows, storage changes, and explicit losses. Allocate proportional bands and put short labels outside flow paths with reserved gaps.\n\nCheck: Check all link weights are nonnegative and use the same units. Verify every intermediate imbalance is explained. Confirm band width scales are consistent across the complete diagram.\n\nPitfalls: Ribbon crossings can encode unavoidable source-destination relationships; never alter weights to remove them. Prefer a source-destination matrix when crossings obscure tracing, and never route ribbons across text.\n\nExample: Trace synthetic energy from three sources through conversion to end uses, explicitly labeling conversion losses and units.\n\nAlternatives: Chord diagram, Alluvial diagram, Waterfall chart."
